A Möbius strip is a fascinating shape with only one side and one continuous edge, made by 
giving a strip of paper a single half-twist (180 degrees) before taping the ends together.
Discovered in 1858 by German mathematicians August Ferdinand Möbius and Johann Benedict Listing, 
it is a classic object in the study of topology.Key PropertiesOne Side: 
If you draw a line down the middle of the strip, it will cover the entire surface and 
return to the start without ever crossing an edge or lifting your pen.
Single Boundary: Unlike a normal loop which has two separate edges (top and bottom), 
a Möbius strip has only one continuous boundary edge.Non-Orientability: 
It is a one-sided surface where 
an ant crawling along the middle would eventually return upside down relative to its starting orientation 
without crossing an edge.What Happens When You Cut It?Cutting down the middle:
Instead of splitting into two separate loops like a normal paper ring, cutting a Möbius strip down its center yields 
one single, much larger loop with extra twists.Cutting one-third of the way across: 
Slicing the strip a third of the way from the edge creates two interlocked, smaller loops.
Real-World Uses Conveyor belts: 
Manufactured as a Möbius strip so that wear and tear distributes evenly across the entire surface area rather than just a single "side," making them last longer.

Recording tapes: Used in early continuous-loop audio and computer printer cartridges to maximize recording tape longevity. 

